RELEASE NOTES, c3p0-0.14.1
==========================

This is a minor release on top of c3p0-0.14.0 to restore
compatibility with Java versions 7, 8, 9, and 10, which
was accidentally broken in c3p0-0.14.0.

The issue is that c3p0-0.14.x generates hard-coded BeanInfo
classes to prevent DataSource.getConnection() and
ConnectionPoolDataSource.getPooledConnection() from getting
treated as JavaBean properties. Calls to those methods are
not safe, combined with maliciously crafted JDBC URLs they
can do harm, but JavaBeans-related libraries promiscuously
and automatically "read" what are presumed to be simple
metadata by calling getXXX() methods. Under c3p0-0.14.x,
attackers can no longer use JavaBean library to provoke
"execution" of maliciously crafted JDBC URLs by simply
having bean libraries run properties-based equality or
ordering tests.

However, these pregenerated BeanInfo classes report all
properties, events, and methods of c3p0 DataSources and
ConnectionPoolDataSources, all that exist in the API under
which the library (and its dependency mchange-commons-java)
are built. Currently that's Java 11, but c3p0 intends to
remain compatible with versions as old as Java 7.
c3p0-0.14.0 naively threw exceptions when expected Java 11
properties, events, or methods proved not to be present
on beans at runtime.

c3p0-0.14.1 no longer breaks when BeanInfo-expected API
is not found in the runtime environment. It simply warns
once into logs what API was not found, then omits the
missing API from BeanInfo reported descriptors.

Thanks to Vlad Skarzhevskyy, @skarzhevskyy on GitHub, for
calling attention to this issue!

